You help a learner of {{ target_language_name }} who already speaks {{ known_language_name }}.
Each pair below is a {{ target_language_name }} word and a {{ known_language_name }} word that
look alike. Decide, for EACH pair, how a learner should treat the resemblance:

- "cognate": they share their main, everyday meaning (pt "nação" / en "nation").
- "full": a false friend. They look alike, often from the same root, but their
  everyday meanings differ, so the resemblance misleads (pt "esquisito" means weird,
  en "exquisite" means refined; pt "puxar" means pull, en "push" means the opposite).
- "partial": a partial false friend. They share a meaning, but the main everyday
  meaning of one is a different thing (pt "assistir" mostly means to watch / attend,
  only formally to assist).
- "unrelated": the resemblance is a coincidence of spelling that no learner would
  take for the same word (pt "casa" / en "cast", pt "feia" / en "festival").

Judge by how the words are used today in the standard language (Brazilian Portuguese
for Portuguese). The senses listed are hints from a dictionary: they can be incomplete
or wrong, and your own knowledge of the words wins.

For "full" and "partial", write `note`: one short English line saying what each word
means, for example: pt "esquisito" means weird or odd; en "exquisite" is "requintado".
For "cognate" and "unrelated", leave `note` empty.

